# TDNS native package bootstrap

The RPM and DEB packages wrap the static binaries published by the matching
immutable GitHub release tag. They do not rebuild TDNS or its Nuxt application
on the target distribution. The package build verifies the selected release
archive against the release checksum file and rejects a binary for the wrong
CPU architecture or version.

The package installs `/usr/bin/tdns`, the `tdns:tdns` system account, a hardened
systemd unit, `/etc/tdns`, and `/var/lib/tdns`. Package installation deliberately
does not create configuration, certificates, private keys, passwords, or bearer
tokens, and it does not enable or start the service.

## Bootstrap

After installation, generate the initial configuration explicitly:

```sh
sudo tdns config \
  --output-dir /etc/tdns \
  --basepath /etc/tdns \
  --data-path /var/lib/tdns \
  --systemd-unit=false
```

The command creates secrets, so restrict them before starting TDNS:

```sh
sudo chown root:tdns /etc/tdns/tdns.yaml /etc/tdns/tdns_key.pem
sudo chmod 0640 /etc/tdns/tdns.yaml /etc/tdns/tdns_key.pem
sudo chown root:root /etc/tdns/tdns_cert.pem
sudo chmod 0644 /etc/tdns/tdns_cert.pem
sudo chown root:tdns /etc/tdns/hostsfile_list
sudo chmod 0640 /etc/tdns/hostsfile_list
sudo systemctl enable --now tdns.service
```

Adjust certificate filenames if a different `--basename` was selected. Review
the generated listeners, certificate hosts, and `dns_access.allowed_client_cidrs`
before enabling a service reachable beyond loopback.

## Package lifecycle

An upgrade restarts TDNS only when the service was already running. Removal
stops and disables the unit but does not delete operator-created files from
`/etc/tdns` or runtime state from `/var/lib/tdns`. System accounts are retained
according to the native distribution policy.

The executable is intentionally static and non-PIE so the same upstream
artifact can be verified and packaged across the supported RPM targets. The
RPM lint configuration documents narrow exceptions for that binary model, the
dynamic service account, and the private directory modes. Manual pages are
generated from the binary's Cobra command tree while the native package is
assembled. Fedora 43 and newer process the packaged sysusers declaration
natively; openSUSE generates the equivalent pre-install script with its
sysusers macro.
